inline_comments.body:
With `skip_non_loss_tokens=True` and an MTP model (tuple `pred`), this skips the block without saying anything. The run takes the full path, so the user doesn't get the speedup they asked for and nothing tells them why. The docstring says "No effect with multiple outputs", which is the warn-and-continue pattern the rules ask us to avoid (CLAUDE.md principle 8, `rules/config.md`). No in-repo config turns the flag on with MTP (none of the RL models has MTP layers), so raising breaks nothing:

```python
            if self.skip_non_loss_tokens:
                if is_multi_output:
                    raise ValueError(
                        "ChunkedLossWrapper.skip_non_loss_tokens does not support multiple "
                        "outputs (MTP)."
                    )
```

Then drop "No effect with multiple outputs (MTP)" from the docstring, or reword it to "Not supported with multiple outputs (MTP)". This raises on the first microbatch, not at config time. A config-time check would need the model config (`mtp_layers`), and `ChunkedLossWrapper.Config` doesn't have it, so this is the smallest place for the check.

Problem: when every token is a loss token (pretraining with ConcatThenSplit and graphs off, e.g. ROCm or `disable_accelerator_graphs=True`), the skip still runs in full:
- an argsort over all tokens;
- per-chunk gathers of every row;
- a [T, D] buffer plus a [T, D] `zeros_like(...).index_copy_` scatter.

The only difference from main is the extra memory and time. Since this is now the default, that cost lands on the main pretraining path.

Fix: after the host sync you already pay, check `num_loss_tokens == num_tokens` and take the `rows = None` path. Main's path then runs unchanged, and the result is bitwise identical to main by construction.

graph_trainer now has three ways to turn the skip off:
- the subclass default (`False`);
- this assignment after the config is built;
- `GraphTrainer.Config.__post_init__`, which raises.

The copy on L372 is what brings `True` back. If you leave the field out of the copy, the subclass default applies, and you can drop this line:

```python
**{f.name: getattr(loss_cfg, f.name) for f in fields(loss_cfg) if f.name != "skip_non_loss_tokens"}
```

Then the `__post_init__` raise is the only check.

